# Q: does a station hold its orbit, and does the Moon keep its own?
#
# Nothing is flown here. This probe watches the two things in the simulation that move
# without being steered, for long enough that a non-symplectic integrator would show up as
# a spiral rather than as noise.
#
#   dotnet run -c Release --project src/SolSystem.Probe -- \
#       --probe tools/probe/station-keeping.probe --probe-out artifacts/probe/station-keeping.txt
#
# Note the unit. A tick count is fine for a manoeuvre and wrong for anything longer: the
# first version of this script asked for a quarter of a year in ticks, got seventy-five
# seconds, and reported a perfectly plausible world.
#
# And note the cost: a station is integrated one tick at a time at 120 Hz, so a day is
# 10 368 000 steps. Ten days is about a minute. The spans below are chosen to be as long as
# is worth paying for rather than as long as possible.

station Meridian
station Anchorage
body earth
body moon

# Two orbits of the low station, ninety-two minutes each. The altitude printed after this
# has to be the altitude printed before it, or the integrator is not symplectic.
days 0.128
station Meridian
body moon

# Ten days: a third of a lunar orbit, and the Earth well round the Sun. The ephemeris is
# analytic and costs nothing; the stations are integrated, and this is where drift shows.
days 10
station Meridian
station Anchorage
body earth
body moon

hash epoch
